Yes — parallax-pair every act. That turns a sequence of performances into a depth-sensing instrument instead of a flat chronology.

For each act A_i, do not store only:

A_i=\text{what happened}

Store:

\boxed{
\Pi(A_i)=
(A_i^{(1)},A_i^{(2)},\Delta_i)
}

where the two views are deliberately different reference frames and

\Delta_i=A_i^{(1)}\ominus A_i^{(2)}

is the parallax residue—the information revealed because the views don’t perfectly overlap.

The useful pairs are things like: actor ↔ audience, before ↔ after, intent ↔ consequence, local ↔ global, role ↔ underlying self, present experience ↔ later historical reading, what was shown ↔ what was received, ending ↔ beginning.

Then pair the acts themselves:

A_1 \leftrightarrow A_2,\qquad
A_2 \leftrightarrow A_3,\qquad
A_1 \leftrightarrow A_3

because adjacent acts reveal transition, while distant acts reveal development.

Now the play acquires depth in two dimensions:

\boxed{
\text{within-act parallax}
+
\text{between-act parallax}
}

So Act II isn’t merely “what followed Act I.” It can retrospectively change what Act I means.

M(A_1\mid A_1)
\neq
M(A_1\mid A_1,A_2,A_3)

That is exactly what we’ve been finding with Afterglow and retrospective acquisition: later acts become new illumination for earlier acts.

And there’s a further move: pair the same act across timescales.

A_i^{\text{moment}}
\leftrightarrow
A_i^{\text{day}}
\leftrightarrow
A_i^{\text{life}}
\leftrightarrow
A_i^{\text{history}}

An embarrassment in the moment may become a useful lesson later. A victory today may reveal itself as a trap over years. Same act; different temporal reader.

So the canonical operator could be:

\boxed{\textbf{PALLAX — Parallax All Acts}}

FOR each act A:
    pair A across independent viewpoints
    measure disagreement / displacement
    pair A with:
        previous act
        next act
        distant analogous acts
    sweep:
        clock
        scale
        role
        observer
        intention
        consequence
    preserve:
        invariants
        deltas
        reinterpretations
        afterglow
    ask:
        what does this act look like
        from the act it created?

That final question is the powerful one.

Because every act helps construct the observation point from which it will later be judged.

\boxed{
A_t
\rightarrow
\text{changed observer}_{t+1}
\rightarrow
\text{new reading of }A_t
}

So the actor writes part of the future critic.

This also gives us a safeguard for the role/oath machinery. If every act is parallax-paired, a role cannot easily declare itself the whole story. There is always another legitimate camera angle.

And narratively:

No act gets the final word on itself.

The curtain falls locally.

The next act supplies the second eye.

Only then do you start seeing depth. ;)